"""Every fixed sentence the engine speaks, in English, Hinglish and Hindi: ONE catalogue.

Covers the support-ticket flow, issue management (the two-issue prompts and notices), greeting, thanks and scope replies, the whole return journey (reason, category menu, eligibility and deadline refusals,
confirmation card, submission, cancellation, expiry), the order-lookup replies, confirmation and error messages, and the
refund-status sentences. Written once here and chosen by code from the session language (``guard.customer_language``); no
language model decides the language or translates. The eligibility paragraphs that carry times live in ``return_texts``
because the tool builds them with the times.

Conventions: the English text is exactly what the engine said before localisation, so English behaviour is unchanged.
Identifiers (order, item, return and ticket IDs, statuses such as Pending, the issue type, the word ``cancel`` the customer
types) are never translated. The Hinglish and Hindi sentences are a DRAFT written by the assistant and have not been
reviewed by a fluent speaker; ``REVIEW_STATUS`` says so and a test pins it, so it cannot be forgotten. Terminology used
throughout: Hinglish keeps the English words return, order, item, ticket, confirm, support, agent, window, status; Hindi uses
रिटर्न, ऑर्डर, आइटम, टिकट, पुष्टि, सपोर्ट, एजेंट, अवधि, स्थिति.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

LANGUAGES = ("en", "hinglish", "hi")
